==English==
{{was wotd|2018|June|23}}

===Etymology===
[[File:Hickman line catheter with 2 lumens.jpg|thumb|upright|A {{w|Hickman line}}, a central [[venous]] catheter used when [[long-term]] [[intravenous]] [[access#Noun|access]] is required for [[chemotherapy]], [[dialysis]], or other uses]]

Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|cathéter}}, from {{der|en|grc|καθετήρ||surgical instrument for emptying the bladder}}, from {{m|grc|καθίημι||to descend, let down}} + {{m|grc|-τήρ||''suffix forming masculine nouns from verbs''}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈkæθɪtə(ɹ)/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|/ˈkæθɪt̬ɚ/|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|ca|the|ter|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|medicine}} A small [[tube]] [[insert#Verb|inserted]] into a [[body]] [[cavity]] to [[administer]] a [[drug#Noun|drug]], [[create]] an [[opening#Noun|opening]], [[distend]] a [[passageway]], or [[remove#Verb|remove]] [[fluid]].
#* {{quote-journal|author=Archibald Cleland|title=XXVI. A Description of a Catheter, Made to Remedy the Inconveniencies Which Occasioned the Leaving Off the High Operation for the Stone; [...]|journal=[[w:Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society|Philosophical Transactions. Giving Some Account of the Present Undertakings, Studies, and Labours, of the Ingenious, in Many Considerable Parts of the World]]|location=London|publisher=Printed for T. Woodward, and C. Davis, {{...|over-against Gray's-Inn-Gate in Holbourn;}} printers to the {{w|Royal Society}}|month=August–December|year=1741|year_published=1744|volume=XLI, part II|page=844|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=gicnaT74pMwC&pg=PA844|passage=And I humbly hope, that the Deſcription, and the Method of uſing this '''''Catheter''''', will be a means of reviving a operation ſo happily begun, and calculated for the Good of thoſe that are afflicted with the Stone in the Bladder.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Samuel Cooper|authorlink=Samuel Cooper (surgeon)|chapter=CATHETER|title=A Dictionary of Practical Surgery:{{nb...|Comprehending All the Most Interesting Improvements up to the Present Period: Also an Account of the Instruments, Remedies, and Applications Employed in Surgery; the Etymology and Signification of the Principal Terms; a Copious Bibliotheca Chirurgica; and a Variety of Original Facts and Observations}}|edition=3rd revised, corrected and enlarged|location=London|publisher=Printed for [[w:Longman|Longman, Hurst, Rees, orme, and Brown]] [''et al.'']|year=1818|page=247|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Bwc_AAAAcAAJ&pg=PA247|column=2|oclc=11288051|passage=Of course there are two kinds of '''catheters''', one intended for the male, the other for the female urethra. The common '''catheter''' is a silver tube, of such diameter as will allow it to be introduced with ease into the urethra, and of various figures and lengths, according as it is intended for the young or adult, the male or female, subject.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Charles Mion|chapter=Practical Use of Peritoneal Dialysis|editor=John F[rancis] Maher|title=Replacement of Renal Function by Dialysis: A Textbook of Dialysis|edition=3rd updated and enlarged|location=Dordrecht|publisher=[[w:Springer Science+Business Media|Kluwer Academic Publishers]]|year=1989|page=540|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=T845qZmgqroC&pg=PA540|isbn=978-0-89838-414-7|passage=Careful positioning of the '''catheter''' is essential to achieve a technically satisfactory dialysis. Final placement is is made by assessing drainage and by positioning the tip of the '''catheter''' according to the patient's comfort.}}

